I’ll first inspect the specimen metadata, pattern range/resolution, and the frozen reference index, then build and refine a complete multiphase GSAS-II model. I’ll keep the deliverables replayable and treat any weakly constrained minor phase or fraction explicitly as uncertain.

The chemistry points first to manganese tungstates plus possible residual Mn oxide or WO₃. I’m narrowing the frozen index to Mn–W–O and precursor compositions, while separately extracting the observed peak families so candidate selection is driven by the specimen rather than chemistry alone.

I’ve confirmed a dominant wolframite MnWO₄ family and a separate WO₃-like family, but several strong low-angle reflections remain outside both. I’m now fitting those two physical phases first in GSAS-II; the resulting difference curve will isolate the residual family before I test precursor oxides or transferred Mn–W–O prototypes.

The residual is not behaving like ordinary tetragonal Mn₃O₄, Mn₂O₃, or MnO₂. Its strongest corrected reflections are a paired low-angle family near 19.9/20.5° plus peaks near 28.8, 32.6, and 35.2°, so I’m testing Mn-substituted tungstate frameworks from the other divalent-metal systems. This is the justified cross-chemistry step: the exact Mn–W–O library is exhausted, while the residual has broad family support and the 1:1 Mn:W synthesis supports another tungstate topology.

The chemically coherent three-phase checkpoint is MnWO₄ + WO₃ + a Mn₃W₂O₉-type oxide; it fits substantially better than precursor-only or high-pressure polymorph branches. I’m now mapping the remaining difference peaks and checking the Mn₃W₂O₉ geometry before committing to the final refinement.

The residual is not yet clean: the 20.88°, 35.64°, and 47.00° peaks form a second coherent family, so the current Mn₃W₂O₉ analogue is incomplete as a structural explanation. I’m screening that family against compositionally plausible indexed references before allowing profile parameters to absorb it.

The residual screen is broad enough to include every Mn/O and W/O reference plus divalent-tungstate analogues, with lattice scaling and the measured displacement applied. This is deliberately a fingerprint pass; any survivor will still have to refine with positive scale and sensible bond geometry.

A tempting W-rich bronze analogue matches several angles, but it violates the Mn:W mass balance once the observed WO₃ is included, so I’m not treating that numerical match as phase evidence. The physically plausible survivors are being tested as full GSAS-II phases, especially Mn-rich oxides that can balance WO₃.

The exact manganese-oxide branches are now refining side-by-side under identical instrument and displacement assumptions. Their relative performance—and whether each keeps a stable positive fraction—is more diagnostic than the earlier stick-pattern ranking.

Several cross-oxide prototypes index subsets, but most require 15–30% isotropic expansion—enough to make Mn–O or W–O distances nonphysical. I’m separating indexing from chemistry now: first establish whether the residual peaks share one lattice, then assign only a chemically and geometrically defensible topology to that lattice.

Autoindexing resolves the main residual family as primitive orthorhombic, approximately 4.44 × 5.03 × 17.03 Å. That is a direct metric match to the Pbcn M₂WO₆ reference family; assigning M = Mn gives chemically plausible Mn₂WO₆ and, unlike the earlier decoys, a Mn-rich phase that can balance residual WO₃.

The residual indexing has isolated a physically plausible Pbcn Mn₂WO₆ family, but it explains only part of the unmatched intensity. I’m now separating the remaining peaks into a second coherent family and will only retain phases whose scale, geometry, and Mn:W balance remain physical in the combined refinement.

The remaining profile is not consistent with a single ordinary manganese oxide: rutile MnO₂ explains the 29.15°/57.0° pair, while bixbyite Mn₂O₃ explains 32.98°/54.91°/65.36°, but neither accounts for the dominant 20.32° and 35.64° peaks. I’m testing whether those belong to a chemically supported mixed Mn–W intermediate rather than forcing several unrelated oxides into the model.

A chemically coherent lead has emerged: a same-stoichiometry M₂WO₆ analogue (orthorhombic P2₁2₁2₁) predicts the full 20.3/20.9, 29.15, 35.6, 36.65, 46–47, and 56–57° family, unlike the earlier Pbcn prototype. I’m constructing the Mn₂WO₆ substitution explicitly and checking its Mn–O/W–O distances before accepting it.

The P2₁2₁2₁ model is positionally credible, but the intensity fit only improves when W moves far enough to create a 1.54 Å W–O contact, so I have rejected that refined checkpoint and rolled back. I’m comparing other M₂WO₆ topologies that preserve sensible 1.7–2.1 Å W–O geometry; this is exactly where a lower Rwp can otherwise select the wrong structure.

The physically valid alternatives (P2/c, P4/nmm, Aea2, and Pbcn Mn₂WO₆) all refine to small positive fractions but leave the dominant family largely intact. I’m reranking the entire same-anion framework library with corrected species substitution—the initial screen had retained oxidation-state-tagged source cations in some analogues, which distorted their calculated intensities.

Mass balance now points more strongly to a ~6 wt% Mn-rich oxide: 18 wt% residual WO₃ requires about 6 wt% Mn₂O₃/Mn₃O₄ to preserve the charged 1:1 Mn:W feed. The common oxide structures account for roughly that mass but not the peak family, so I’m treating the family as a possible nonstandard manganese-oxide polymorph and screening oxide frameworks with the corrected Mn substitution.

The oxide analogues fit only by taking 9–16 wt%, which would make Mn:W ≈1.3–1.4 and contradict the weighed 1:1 feed. A ~15 wt% Mn₃WO₆ intermediate, however, exactly balances ~15 wt% WO₃ while keeping the dominant MnWO₄; I’m now enumerating that stoichiometry’s physically valid frameworks rather than accepting the oxide-only numerical fits.

The two dominant phases are secure (MnWO₄ and monoclinic WO₃). I’m now testing the residual family against Mn₃WO₆-compatible ordered oxide frameworks—the side phase most consistent with both the unindexed peaks and the nominal 1:1 Mn:W balance—before committing the final inventory.

The residual is not random: its strongest lines can be indexed together, but several simple Mn-oxide references also mimic subsets of them. I’m testing those jointly now; a genuine phase should retain a positive scale and account for a broad reflection family when the competing patterns are present, not just capture one or two strong peaks.

The joint MnO/MnO₂ fit confirms they are decoys: they improve isolated peaks but imply an implausible 800 °C oxide assemblage and a large Mn:W imbalance. I’m keeping the search centered on a W-bearing Mn-rich phase and using the full residual family, not the single-peak rankings.

The broad analogue search is now including tier-2 oxide frameworks (for example, tellurate/molybdate and ordered double-perovskite relatives), with Mn/W reassigned and geometry checked after cell scaling. This is the key test for a withheld Mn₃WO₆-type structure.

A physical R‑3 Mn₃WO₆ model derived from a Mn/Cu tellurate is supported at roughly 6–7 wt%, but it does not account for the strongest pseudocubic residual family. I’m therefore checking whether that family belongs to a separate charge-balanced Mn₂WO₆ or Mn₃W₂O₉ framework before deciding whether the R‑3 phase is real or merely overlapping.

The NASICON-like Mn₂WO₆ topology gives the best positional match so far (Rwp ≈16.1% before coordinate adjustment), but direct refinement drives Mn–O/W–O bonds too short. I’m treating that numerical gain as provisional and testing whether the same topology has any physically valid coordinate basin; if it does not, it will be rejected.

The accepted four-phase model now closes the Mn:W balance to within about 10% and all four phases retain positive scales: MnWO₄, WO₃, NASICON-topology Mn₂WO₆, and R‑3 Mn₃WO₆. Both adapted structures pass local bond-length checks after bounded refinement. I’m now refining only supported profile/specimen terms from that physical checkpoint.

The four-phase inventory is now chemically and crystallographically coherent. I’m checking whether the last profile terms genuinely improve the fit or merely push parameters to bounds, then I’ll freeze the most defensible checkpoint and generate the requested replayable deliverables.

The first texture test gives only a modest improvement (Rwp 12.542% → 12.477%) with an interior, physically allowed parameter. I’m screening the few symmetry-distinct axes now; I’ll retain texture only if the improvement is broad and stable, since the current residual is dominated by relative-intensity mismatch rather than unindexed peaks.

Cell re-refinement was stable and improved Rwp to 12.492%, with only small lattice changes. The remaining largest mismatches coincide with reflections of the two minor phases, so I’m doing one final bounded coordinate pass within the already validated coordination basins; this is preferable to adding another phase because no coherent unindexed family remains.

The bounded minor-phase coordinate adjustment remained chemically physical, but its apparent lowest Rwp was not fully replayable after GSAS-II regenerated the profile. I discarded that numerical artifact. The converged replay is stable at Rwp 12.352%, with essentially unchanged phase fractions on a second pass; this is the checkpoint I’ll submit.

The submission files are now generated from the stable project. I’m doing the final consistency audit: replaying a copy of `final.gpx`, matching its fractions and calculated profile against `result.json`/`fit.csv`, and screening every exported CIF for composition and short-contact errors.

The final CIF audit caught one unacceptable detail before submission: the initial WO3 reference contains a 1.58 Å O–O contact despite matching the peak positions. I’m replacing that coordinate set with a physical monoclinic WO3 reference at the same lattice metric and re-refining its bounded oxygen coordinates; the current artifacts will be regenerated afterward.

The physical WO3 candidates are being compared in the identical four-phase refinement, including transformed-cell P2₁/c and triclinic variants. This is a strict same-composition substitution test; the colliding reference will not be retained even if its nominal Rwp is slightly lower.

The replacement P2₁/c WO3 model is fully physical (minimum O–O 2.54 Å) and actually improves the stable replay to Rwp 12.051%. I’m doing a small fixed-profile sensitivity scan around that checkpoint; unlike the earlier bound-driven branches, every tested project is independently recalculated and replayable.

Completed and validated with a fresh GSAS-II replay.

- MnWO₄: 72.18 wt%
- WO₃: 15.31 wt%
- Mn₂WO₆: 6.96 wt%
- Mn₃WO₆: 5.56 wt%
- Rwp: 12.039%; Rp: 6.834%

The two minor-phase structures and their individual fraction split remain provisional; details are in the report.

